// Where an approved intention becomes the one payload a model may
// receive. Both libraries above are spliced in whole and are wired
// together here, which is the only place they can meet:
// src/lib/chunk.ts reduces the material behind a candidate to a
// bounded chunk and refuses the ones that are not worth a call, and
// src/lib/prompt-frame.ts takes that chunk and fences it so that
// everything inside the fence is read as data and never as
// instructions. Nothing below states a reduction rule or a framing
// rule of its own.
//
// AND NOTHING ELSE GOES FORWARD, which is the whole reason this
// node exists as a separate one. The item it answers carries the
// two framed halves, the four ids a row below is written against,
// the ids of the documents the pass offered, and the measurements
// of the prompt. Neither the candidate's name nor its terms nor a
// document body survives it. So no expression below can reach for
// the stored text, and the model node is fed a prepared chunk
// because there is nothing else on the item to feed it.
//
// THE TRUSTED HALF AND THE UNTRUSTED HALF ARRIVE AS TWO MEMBERS,
// which is the framing library's structural claim rather than a
// convenience. A caller wiring a model node has to decide which
// channel each half goes in, and two members make that decision
// visible on this canvas: the node below puts system in the system
// message and data in the prompt, and neither can be confused for
// the other by an expression that reached for one string.
//
// THE PERSONA IS A ROW AND NEVER A LITERAL. It arrives from the
// domain's personas, fetched by Load Domain Context at the top of
// this pass, and personas in src/db/schema/domains.ts argues at
// length why no prompt string is written into a workflow file at
// all: one file serves every domain, the text is what an operator
// tunes most, and a prompt fixed on a canvas is lost at the next
// import. The role read is the researcher, which is the role that
// reads documents, and it is the same role ar-ingest frames
// against. The two ask different things of it and neither writes
// the ask down here.
//
// THERE IS A HEADER, AND THAT IS WHERE THIS PARTS FROM ar-ingest.
// buildChunk takes a roster of fields and the values already
// resolved for them, and writes a header line per field so a model
// does not have to guess what is known. At ingest nothing has
// resolved a field yet, so that node passes no roster and says so.
// Here three things are known before the call and every one of them
// is a fact about the ask rather than a guess about the answer: the
// subject a person approved research on, the search terms they
// approved with it, and the ids of the documents this pass is
// handing over. Writing them into the header is the difference
// between asking about a candidate and asking about a body of text
// that happens to mention one.
//
// THE OFFERED IDS ARE IN THE HEADER BECAUSE AN ANSWER IS JUDGED
// AGAINST THEM. researchBriefErrors in src/lib/research-brief.ts
// refuses an answer whose citations name a document the pass never
// offered, and a model that was never told the ids can only guess
// at them. So the header states the set the answer will be held to.
//
// WHAT A CITATION CAN AND CANNOT BE WRONG ABOUT is worth saying
// rather than implying. Each document is introduced in the body by
// a marker this node writes, and the text after that marker is
// somebody else's, so a document is free to spell a marker of its
// own. What that buys is a mis-attribution INSIDE the offered set,
// which the record group cannot tell from a genuine one. What it
// cannot buy is a citation outside that set: the offered ids
// travel on the item, the answer is judged against them, and a
// forged id naming a document this pass never handed over is
// refused with the row left unstamped.
//
// A DOCUMENT WITH NO TEXT IS NOT OFFERED. The drain carries
// parse_status beside every body precisely so this node can tell
// an empty body from a failed parse, and both come to the same
// thing for the composition: a document whose body reduced to
// nothing was never handed over, so its id is not among the
// offered ones and an answer citing it is refused. A parse that
// FAILED and still left text is offered, with the marker saying
// so, because partial text is still text somebody captured and
// hiding that from a reader would be worse than labelling it.
//
// A CANDIDATE WITH NOTHING BEHIND IT IS SKIPPED RATHER THAN
// ASKED ABOUT. The drain says an empty documents list is a real
// state, and buildChunk refuses a body that reduced to nothing.
// This node does not set allowFieldsOnly, and the omission is the
// decision: nothing on this canvas reaches outside the corpus, so
// a call made with a subject line and no material would be a call
// answered out of the model's own parameters, and the result would
// be stored as this domain's research with nothing behind it. The
// three identifying flags a fields-only chunk would be judged by
// are left off the roster below for the same reason: they govern
// that flag alone and this node never sets it.
//
// THE EXCERPT CEILING IS THE LIBRARY'S AND IT IS SHARED. All the
// bodies of one candidate are composed into a single excerpt, and
// that excerpt meets MAX_EXCERPT_CHARS exactly as one document's
// does at ingest. So five documents behind a candidate share the
// bound that one document has to itself, which is a real cost and
// the right one: what is capped is what a call is charged for, and
// the cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the number of rows
// it was assembled from.
//
// TWO REFUSALS, and they are answered differently because they are
// faults of different things. A chunk refusal is about one
// candidate: nothing survived the boilerplate removal, or what
// survived is too short to be worth reading. That candidate is
// skipped and the pass carries on. A persona refusal is about the
// operator's own text, is identical for every candidate in the
// pass, and would otherwise be a run that quietly made no calls at
// all, so it stops the pass by name. Which of the three the framing
// library refused is what parts them, and CHUNK_EMPTY_REASON is the
// one that belongs to the candidate.
//
// WHAT A SKIPPED CANDIDATE LOSES, and it is less than a skipped
// document loses at ingest. Nothing between the drain and the
// record group writes a row, so a candidate that does not reach a
// call is stamped by nothing: it keeps its approval and its open
// researched_at and is fetched first again by the next pass. A
// candidate skipped because the corpus behind it holds no prose is
// a candidate that will be skipped again until something upstream
// captures material for it, and that repetition costs a place at
// the head of the queue and nothing else.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in, at most one out, and fewer whenever a
// chunk was refused. Nothing below pairs against the node above
// this one: every item carries its own pool_id, and the ledger
// below pairs against THIS node by position.

// The role whose system text this workflow speaks with.
// personas.role is free text and carries no CHECK, the roles a
// pipeline plays growing with the pipeline, so this is a lookup
// into rows an operator wrote rather than a member of a closed set.
// The seed writes three: this one reads documents, and ar-ingest
// reads the same row to ask a different question of it.
const PERSONA_ROLE = 'researcher';

// The header roster, in the order buildChunk writes it. No entry
// carries an identifying flag, and the omission is deliberate:
// that flag governs allowFieldsOnly alone, which this node never
// sets, so writing one would be configuration nothing reads.
const RESEARCH_FIELDS = [
  {
    key: 'subject',
    label: 'Subject',
    fallback: '(the intention names no subject)',
  },
  {
    key: 'terms',
    label: 'Approved search terms',
    fallback: '(the intention names no terms)',
  },
  {
    key: 'documents',
    label: 'Documents offered, by id',
    fallback: '(no documents offered)',
  },
];

// What separates the members of a list inside one header line.
// buildChunk collapses every value to a single line, so this is
// about what a reader and a model can both parse rather than about
// layout.
const LIST_SEPARATOR = ', ';

// What separates one document from the next in the composed body.
const DOCUMENT_SEPARATOR = '\n\n';

// A document id in the one spelling an answer will be judged
// against. canonicalId in src/lib/research-brief.ts is the
// authority and this is a second copy of its rule, the splice
// forbidding the import that would share one, so a change there is
// an edit here. Both halves are the rule. A number is read only
// when it is a positive safe integer, past which the value has
// already lost digits and names no document. A string is read only
// in the canonical decimal spelling, so 012 is refused rather than
// read as twelve and one document cannot arrive under two ids.
function canonicalDocumentId(value) {
  if (typeof value === 'number') {
    return Number.isSafeInteger(value) && value > 0
      ? String(value)
      : null;
  }

  if (typeof value !== 'string') {
    return null;
  }

  return /^[1-9][0-9]*$/.test(value)
    ? value
    : null;
}

// The documents this pass is handing over, in the order the drain
// answered them, cut to the ones there is text to hand over. A row
// whose body reduced to nothing was never offered, so its id stays
// out of the header and an answer citing it is refused downstream.
// parse_status decides how a document is LABELLED and never
// whether it is offered: text a failed parse left behind is still
// text somebody captured.
function offeredDocuments(value) {
  const offered = [];

  if (!Array.isArray(value)) {
    return offered;
  }

  for (const entry of value) {
    if (entry === null || typeof entry !== 'object') {
      continue;
    }

    const id = canonicalDocumentId(entry.id);
    const body = typeof entry.body === 'string'
      ? entry.body.trim()
      : '';

    if (id === null || body === '') {
      continue;
    }

    offered.push({ id, body, parsed: entry.parse_status === 'ok' });
  }

  return offered;
}

// How one document is introduced in the composed body, and the
// whole of what a model is told about where a passage came from.
// The marker is trusted text this node writes; everything after it
// is somebody else's.
function documentBlock(entry) {
  const marker = entry.parsed
    ? '[document ' + entry.id + ']'
    : '[document ' + entry.id + ', partial parse]';

  return marker + '\n' + entry.body;
}

// The approved terms as one line. research_pool.search_terms is a
// jsonb list and nothing has validated its members, so a member
// that is not text is dropped rather than coerced into a header
// line, and an empty list leaves the roster's stand-in standing.
function termsText(value) {
  if (!Array.isArray(value)) {
    return '';
  }

  const terms = [];

  for (const term of value) {
    if (typeof term === 'string' && term.trim() !== '') {
      terms.push(term.trim());
    }
  }

  return terms.join(LIST_SEPARATOR);
}

// The system text of the persona holding a role, or the empty
// string where there is none. Every member is typed on the way
// out, because these entries came back through jsonb and nothing
// has validated them: a row whose system_text is not text is a row
// an operator has not finished, and it refuses below with the same
// sentence a missing row does.
function personaText(entries, role) {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' && entry.role === role
      && typeof entry.system_text === 'string') {
      return entry.system_text;
    }
  }

  return '';
}

const context = $('Load Domain Context').first().json;
const personas = Array.isArray(context.personas)
  ? context.personas
  : [];
const persona = personaText(personas, PERSONA_ROLE);

// A pass with no researcher persona, or one whose text is not
// text. Both are the same fault from here and both are pass wide,
// so this refuses before a single chunk is built rather than once
// per candidate.
if (persona === '') {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-research] Prepare Model Prompt has no system text for the '
    + PERSONA_ROLE + ' persona: the domain states none, or the row '
    + 'it states holds no text',
  );
}

const items = [];

for (const item of $input.all()) {
  const row = item.json;
  const offered = offeredDocuments(row.documents);
  const ids = offered.map((entry) => entry.id);
  const built = buildChunk({
    fields: RESEARCH_FIELDS,
    values: {
      subject: row.entity_name,
      terms: termsText(row.search_terms),
      documents: ids.join(LIST_SEPARATOR),
    },
    body: offered.map(documentBlock).join(DOCUMENT_SEPARATOR),
  });

  if (!built.usable) {
    continue;
  }

  const framed = promptFrame(persona, built.chunk);

  if (!framed.usable) {
    // A framing refusal that is not about the chunk is about the
    // persona, and a persona fault repeats on every candidate in
    // the pass. Refusing by name here is what parts an operator
    // text fault from a queue with nothing readable behind it.
    if (framed.reason !== CHUNK_EMPTY_REASON) {
      throw new Error(
        '[ar-research] Prepare Model Prompt cannot frame the '
        + PERSONA_ROLE + ' persona: ' + framed.reason,
      );
    }

    continue;
  }

  const promptChars = framed.system.length + framed.data.length;

  // What the model node is fed, and what the two nodes after it
  // read. The name is gone, the terms are gone and no body is
  // here: the fenced form is the only spelling of any of them that
  // leaves this node.
  //
  // offered_ids is the exception and is not one of them. It is a
  // list of ids this node derived, not a member the drain handed
  // over, and it travels because the record group judges the
  // answer's citations against exactly this set. Deriving it a
  // second time down there would be a second statement of what was
  // offered, and two statements of that can disagree.
  items.push({
    json: {
      run_id: row.run_id,
      pool_id: row.pool_id,
      domain_id: row.domain_id,
      entity_id: row.entity_id,
      offered_ids: ids,
      system: framed.system,
      data: framed.data,
      prompt_chars: promptChars,
      est_tokens: estimateTokens(promptChars),
      fence_cuts: framed.fenceCuts,
      forms_defanged: framed.formsDefanged,
    },
  });
}

return items;

